@prefix ldh:    <https://w3id.org/atomgraph/linkeddatahub#> .
@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix dh:     <https://www.w3.org/ns/ldt/document-hierarchy#> .
@prefix dct:    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<> a dh:Item ;
    dct:title "Import CSV data" ;
    dct:description "Step-by-step guide to converting CSV data to RDF and importing it into the end-user application" ;
    rdf:_1 <#content> .

<#content> a ldh:XHTML ;
    rdf:value """<div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
    <p class="lead">Step-by-step guide to converting CSV data to RDF and importing it into the end-user application</p>
    <p>A CSV import has three components:</p>
    <dl>
        <dt>CSV file</dt>
        <dd>The file containing the tabular data</dd>
        <dt>Mapping query</dt>
        <dd>SPARQL <code>CONSTRUCT</code> query that maps CSV rows to an RDF representation</dd>
        <dt>CSV import</dt>
        <dd>Combines the file with the corresponding query</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="ldhc-tabs or-horizontal">
        <div class="ldhc-tablist sz-md va-line" role="tablist">
            <button aria-controls="user-interface-pane" aria-selected="true" class="ldhc-tab is-on" id="user-interface-tab" role="tab" type="button"><span class="ldhc-tab-lbl">User interface</span></button>
            <button aria-controls="command-line-interface-pane" aria-selected="false" class="ldhc-tab" id="command-line-interface-tab" role="tab" type="button"><span class="ldhc-tab-lbl">Command line interface</span></button>
        </div>
        <div aria-labelledby="user-interface-tab" class="ldhc-tabpanel" id="user-interface-pane" role="tabpanel">
                <div>
                    <div class="screenshot-placeholder">
                        <span aria-hidden="true" class="msi">play_circle</span>
                        <p>Screen recording: importing CSV data and mapping it to RDF</p>
                    </div>
                    <p>All the import components can be created from a single dialog, but the steps below create them one at a time:</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>First, create a container for the data to be imported. This will be the import's <dfn>target container</dfn>.</li>
                        <li>Upload a CSV file. Click on the <span class="btn btn-primary create-action">Create <span class="caret"></span></span> dropdown in the top-left corner and select
                                <dfn>File</dfn>. This will open a new form.</li>
                        <li>Fill out the <dfn>Item</dfn> fields — the document's <samp>Title</samp> (required) and optional <samp>Description</samp>, the same fields used when <a href="../../create-data/create-documents/">creating a document</a>.</li>
                        <li>
                            Fill out the mandatory <dfn>File</dfn> fields:
                            <dl>
                                <dt>Filename</dt>
                                <dd>Click on <dfn>Choose file</dfn> and select a CSV file from your computer</dd>
                                <dt>Title</dt>
                                <dd>File title</dd>
                            </dl>
                        </li>
                        <li>Once all the fields have been filled in, click <span class="btn btn-primary btn-save">Save</span>.</li>
                        <li>The browser will open your imported file.</li>
                        <li>Create a <a href="../../../reference/imports/csv/#vocabulary-conversion">mapping query</a> that will
                            transform the uploaded CSV to RDF — the <a href="../../../tutorial/data/">tutorial's Data stage</a> walks through a complete example, <samp>categories.rq</samp>.
                            Click on the <span class="btn btn-primary create-action">Create <span class="caret"></span></span> dropdown in the top-left corner and
                            select <dfn>CONSTRUCT</dfn>. This will open a new form.</li>
                        <li>Fill out the <samp>Item</samp> fields.</li>
                        <li>
                            Fill out the mandatory <dfn>Query</dfn> fields:
                            <dl>
                                <dt>Text</dt>
                                <dd>SPARQL <code>CONSTRUCT</code> query string</dd>
                                <dd><em>You will not be able to save the query if its syntax is not valid SPARQL 1.1.</em></dd>
                                <dt>Title</dt>
                                <dd>Query title</dd>
                            </dl>
                        </li>
                        <li>Once all the fields have been filled in, click <span class="btn btn-primary btn-save">Save</span>. You can find it saved in the
                                <dfn>Queries</dfn> container, which you can open from the <a href="../../../reference/user-interface/#document-hierarchy">document tree</a> in the left sidebar.</li>
                        <li>Click on the <span class="btn btn-primary create-action">Create <span class="caret"></span></span> dropdown button in the top-left corner and select <dfn>CSV Import</dfn>.
                                This will open a new form.</li>
                        <li>Fill out the <samp>Item</samp> fields.</li>
                        <li>
                            Fill out the mandatory <dfn>CSV Import</dfn> fields:
                            <dl>
                                <dt>File</dt>
                                <dd>Start typing the name of your CSV file and select it from the typeahead</dd>
                                <dt>Query</dt>
                                <dd>Select the mapping query using the typeahead</dd>
                                <dt>Title</dt>
                                <dd>Import title</dd>
                            </dl>
                        </li>
                        <li>Click <span class="btn btn-primary btn-save">Save</span> and you will be redirected to the newly created import document.</li>
                        <li>Reload the document after a few moments to see the import progress. On success, the amount of data imported is
                                displayed; on failure, the constraint violations or other import errors.</li>
                        <li>Go back to the target container to see the newly imported data items.</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
            </div>
        <div aria-labelledby="command-line-interface-tab" class="ldhc-tabpanel" hidden="hidden" id="command-line-interface-pane" role="tabpanel">
                <div>
                    <p>The import is described by a document, which the command takes as its last argument, so create that document first. With the <a href="../../../reference/command-line-interface/">CLI</a> installed and the <samp>LDH_*</samp> variables set:</p>
                    <pre>doc=$(ldh create-item \\
    --title "Categories import" \\
    --container "${LDH_BASE}categories/")

ldh imports import-csv \\
    --title "Categories" \\
    --query-file categories/categories.rq \\
    --csv-file categories/categories.csv \\
    "$doc"</pre>
                    <p class="exhibit-links">Source: <a href="https://github.com/AtomGraph/LinkedDataHub-Apps/blob/master/demo/northwind-traders/categories/categories.rq" target="_blank">categories.rq</a>, <a href="https://github.com/AtomGraph/LinkedDataHub-Apps/blob/master/demo/northwind-traders/categories/categories.csv" target="_blank">categories.csv</a> — the mapping and the data are walked through in the <a href="../../../reference/imports/csv/">CSV imports reference</a> and the <a href="../../../tutorial/data/">tutorial's Data stage</a></p>
                    <p><code>--delimiter</code> defaults to <samp>,</samp> and can be set to another character.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
    </div>
    <p class="lead"><a href="../../../reference/imports/csv/" target="_blank">Read more about how CSV imports work.</a></p>
</div>"""^^rdf:XMLLiteral .
